+---
+id: dl-001-keep-vat-on-shipping-anna-s-report-is-not-a-bug
+type: decision-log
+title: "keep VAT on shipping; Anna's report is not a bug"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+A customer, Anna, reported that her receipt charges VAT on delivery (€1.08 on €4.90 of shipping) and
+argued that delivery is a service, not goods, so it should not carry VAT. This arrived alongside two
+genuine bugs (coupon/volume-discount stacking, inclusive coupon expiry), bundled as one batch of
+"reports about the order service".
+
+`README.md` (the authoritative business rules for this service) states explicitly: "Shipping is
+subject to VAT at the standard rate (22%), shown on its own line of the receipt," and "The order total
+is goods + shipping + VAT on goods + VAT on shipping." The code (`src/tax.ts: shippingVat`,
+`src/pricing.ts`) implements exactly this, and it is already covered by an existing passing test
+(`test/orders.test.ts`, "prices a small order"). €4.90 × 22% = €1.078, which rounds half up to €1.08 —
+precisely the amount Anna saw.
+
+## Decision
+
+Treat Anna's report as a mismatch between her expectation and the documented, intentional business
+rule, not as a defect. No code change was made for this report. The repository instructs that when
+code and the README disagree the README is right; here code and README agree with each other, so there
+is nothing to reconcile unilaterally.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- No change to `src/tax.ts` or `src/pricing.ts` for this report; existing tests continue to cover the
+  behaviour.
+- Whether delivery should in fact be VAT-exempt is a tax/product policy question (and may have a real
+  legal answer depending on jurisdiction), not something to decide inside a bug-fix task. If the
+  business wants to change this, it needs a deliberate rule change to the README's VAT section plus a
+  corresponding code change, not a silent fix.
+- The customer-facing answer is: the current VAT treatment of shipping is correct per policy, not an
+  error on the order.
